Some rules restate, in their comments and messages, the advice they enforce.
Each rule links to its source. Sources and their terms:

  - William Strunk Jr., The Elements of Style (1918), Project Gutenberg
    ebook #37134 (https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/37134), in the public
    domain. The Strunk style transcribes Rules 10, 11, and 13 and the
    entries of Chapter V that name a replacement; its comments quote the
    book.
  - Elmore Leonard, "Easy on the Adverbs, Exclamation Points and Especially
    Hooptedoodle", The New York Times, July 16, 2001, reprinted in The
    Guardian's "Ten rules for writing fiction" (February 20, 2010). The
    Leonard style implements six of the ten rules; each is restated in the
    package's own words, with short quotations in rule comments.
  - Chuck Palahniuk, "Nuts and Bolts: 'Thought' Verbs", LitReactor, August
    12, 2013. The Palahniuk style takes its verb list from the essay, with a
    short quotation in the rule comment.
  - George Orwell, "Politics and the English Language", Horizon, April
    1946. The Orwell style implements four of the six rules, with the
    essay's own lists of dying metaphors, verbal false limbs, pretentious
    diction, and foreign phrases; each rule comment quotes the passage it
    comes from.
  - The Hemingway Editor (https://hemingwayapp.com), whose help pages
    describe the highlights the Hemingway style reproduces: adverbs,
    passive voice, qualifiers, words with simpler alternatives, hard and
    very hard sentences, and the reading grade. Rule comments quote the
    help pages briefly. The word lists are the package's own, except
    Simpler, which takes its pairs from PlainLanguage.gov, "Use simple
    words and phrases" (archived at
    https://github.com/GSA/plainlanguage.gov/blob/main/_pages/guidelines/words/use-simple-words-phrases.md),
    a work of the United States government in the public domain.

The core Fiction and Narration styles are the package's own. Elmore
Leonard, Chuck Palahniuk, William Strunk Jr., and George Orwell are named
as the authors of the advice the styles carry, and Hemingway names the
editor whose checks the style follows; this package is not endorsed by or
affiliated with any of them, their estates, or the editor's makers.
